---
/* WHERE IT LANDS
   The hero claims the text arrives in the window you already had focus in, and
   until now the page never showed it. A logo row normally reads as an
   integration list, which is the exact claim the schematic below disproves, so
   the note under it says what these are instead.

   Zero JS. The sketch built these fifteen list items at runtime and appended a
   second copy of them for the roll; both halves are printed here instead, and
   the reduced-motion rule in site.css hides the duplicate. That is the same
   picture from a stylesheet rather than from a media query sampled once at
   load, so a reader who changes the setting gets the change. */
import { FOCUS_APPS } from '../lib/focusApps';
---
<section class="focus" aria-labelledby="focus-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="focus__h" id="focus-h">It lands in whatever window has focus.</p>
    <div class="focus__rail">
      {/* The roll translates by exactly -50%, so the row has to be two
          identical halves or it jumps at the seam. */}
      <ul class="focus__row is-rolling" data-focus-row>
        {[0, 1].map((half) => FOCUS_APPS.map((app) => (
          <li
            style={app.colour ? `--c:${app.colour}` : undefined}
            aria-hidden={half === 1 ? 'true' : undefined}
          >
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d={app.path} /></svg>
            <span>{app.label}</span>
          </li>
        )))}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p class="focus__note">Not integrations. There is nothing to install into any of
      these: WordScript types where you type.</p>
  </div>
</section>
